---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import PageIntro from '../../components/PageIntro.astro';
import Section from '../../components/Section.astro';
import VsTable from '../../components/VsTable.astro';
import { REPO } from '../../content/site';
import Faq from '../../components/Faq.astro';
import { faqPageLd } from '../../content/readme';

const title = 'hippo vs Cognee: zero-dependency local memory vs graph stack';
const description =
  'hippo and Cognee compared on 22 November 2026. Cognee builds knowledge graphs across SQLite, LanceDB and Ladybug (or Neo4j and Postgres); hippo runs as one zero-dependency SQLite store with markdown mirrors.';
const rows = [
  ['Decay by default', 'Decay by default'],
  ['Retrieval strengthening', 'Recall strengthens a memory'],
  ['Outcome tracking', 'Outcome tracking'],
  ['Conflict detection + resolution', 'Conflict detection'],
  ['Schema acceleration / knowledge graph', 'Knowledge graph'],
  ['Multi-agent shared memory', 'Shared between agents'],
  ['Auto-hook install', 'Installs its own hooks'],
  ['MCP server', 'MCP server'],
  ['Zero runtime deps', 'Zero runtime dependencies'],
  ['Git-friendly', 'Git-friendly'],
  ['License', 'Licence'],
] as const;

const qa = [
  {
    q: 'How does hippo compare with Cognee for coding agents?',
    a: 'Cognee is a Python knowledge-graph engine that coordinates SQLite, LanceDB and Ladybug locally (or Neo4j and Postgres for multi-process sharing) and extracts entities via an LLM when an API key is set. Hippo is a zero-dependency MIT CLI backed by one SQLite file plus markdown mirrors that multiple coding agents share directly with zero LLM calls on save.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can multiple coding agents share one local store without running Neo4j or Postgres?',
    a: 'Yes. Hippo stores memories in a single SQLite file with WAL concurrency in .hippo/ so Claude Code, Codex, Cursor and MCP clients read and write the same repo store safely, and hippo serve shares one store across a team with no external graph database.',
  },
];
---

<Base title={title} description={description} crumb="hippo vs Cognee" published="2026-11-22" schema={[faqPageLd(qa)]}>
  <Nav />
  <main id="main">
    <PageIntro>
      <Fragment slot="title">hippo vs <span class="text-ink">Cognee</span></Fragment>
      <p>
        Both are open-source memory engines with Claude Code, Codex and MCP integrations. Cognee is a Python knowledge-graph
        platform built for documents, ontologies and enterprise data pipelines across three separate stores. Hippo is a
        zero-dependency Node.js CLI purpose-built for coding agents, using one SQLite file with readable markdown mirrors
        and zero language-model calls on save or recall.
      </p>
    </PageIntro>

    <Section title="One SQLite file vs a three-database graph stack">
      <p>
        Locally, Cognee coordinates three embedded stores: SQLite for relational metadata, LanceDB for vectors and Ladybug
        for its embedded graph. Cognee's own docs note that local files cannot be shared across multiple processes, so
        multi-agent or production sharing requires external services such as Neo4j and Postgres/PGVector behind an API
        service (<a class="link" href="https://docs.cognee.ai/setup-configuration/graph-stores.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cognee graph-store docs<span class="sr-only"> (opens in new tab)</span></a>, checked 22 November 2026).
      </p>
      <p>
        Hippo keeps everything in one SQLite store per repository (plus <code>~/.hippo/</code> globally) alongside plain
        markdown mirrors you can commit to git. Multiple coding agents read and write the same store on your machine, and
        <code>hippo serve</code> shares one store across a team with API keys, roles and an audit log.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Feature by feature" alt>
      <p>
        The Cognee column was checked against Cognee's repository and docs on 22 November 2026. The full table is on
        <a class="link" href={`${REPO}#comparison`} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub<span class="sr-only"> (opens in new tab)</span></a>.
      </p>
      <VsTable system="Cognee" rows={rows} />
    </Section>

    <Section title="What Cognee ships today">
      <p>
        Cognee's core is Apache-2.0 on Python 3.10 to 3.14. With <code>LLM_API_KEY</code> set, <code>cognify()</code>
        runs language-model extraction per chunk or batch to build entity-relationship graphs; without a key, v1.6.0+
        falls back to a local CPU <code>gliner_demo</code> extractor and local embeddings. Cognee Cloud and Enterprise
        BYOC add managed workspaces, auto-ontologies and a licensed Postgres graph adapter (<a class="link" href="https://github.com/topoteretes/cognee" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cognee repo<span class="sr-only"> (opens in new tab)</span></a>,
        <a class="link" href="https://www.cognee.ai/pricing" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">pricing<span class="sr-only"> (opens in new tab)</span></a>, checked 22 November 2026).
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="When to pick which" alt>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-rule bg-paper-2 p-6">
          <h3 class="text-base font-medium text-ink">Pick hippo</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            Pick hippo when you want fast, zero-dependency coding-agent memory in one SQLite file, zero LLM calls on save,
            git-reviewable rules, outcome weakening and verified repeat-mistake reduction.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-rule bg-paper-2 p-6">
          <h3 class="text-base font-medium text-ink">Pick Cognee</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            Pick Cognee when you are building a Python GraphRAG pipeline over PDFs, audio, databases and custom OWL/RDF
            ontologies, and running Neo4j and Postgres fits your architecture.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3 pt-4">
        <a href="/quickstart/" class="btn">Get started with hippo</a>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Faq items={qa} heading="hippo and Cognee, asked directly" moreHref="/faq/" />
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>
